hadiths[53]{hadithnumber,text}:
"919","Narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Fasting is a shield (against the punishment of Allah), so the fasting person should not utter obscene words nor speak ignorantly (such as joking, lying, shouting, or making noise, etc.), and if someone fights with him or abuses him, he should say twice, 'I am fasting.' By the One in Whose hand is my soul, the smell from the mouth of a fasting person is better with Allah than the fragrance of musk. (Allah says:) The fasting person leaves his food, drink, and desires for My sake. (So) fasting is for Me, and I alone will give its reward, and every good deed is rewarded tenfold (but the reward for fasting is much greater than this)."
"920","Sahih Sahl (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""In Paradise, there is a gate called 'Rayyan.' On the Day of Resurrection, only those who fast will enter through it; no one else will enter through that gate. It will be announced: 'Where are those who fasted?' Then they will stand up, and no one except them will enter through that gate. When they have entered, the gate will be closed, and no one else will enter through it."
"921","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever spends two pairs (of clothes or any two things) in the way of Allah will be called from the gates of Paradise (the angels will say): O servant of Allah! This gate is good (enter through it). Then whoever is among those who establish prayer will be called from the gate of prayer, and whoever is among those who strive in jihad will be called from the gate of jihad, and whoever is among those who fast will be called from 'Bab al-Rayyan (Rayyan)', and whoever is among those who give charity will be called from the gate of charity."" So Sayyiduna Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah! May my parents be sacrificed for you, whoever is called from all these gates will have no further need (left). Will anyone be called from all these gates?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Yes! And I hope that you will be among them."
"922","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When Ramadan comes, the gates of Paradise are opened."
"923","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) in another narration says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When Ramadan comes, the doors of the sky (that is, Paradise) are opened and the doors of Hell are closed, and the devils are chained with the strongest chains."
"924","Narrated Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""When you see the (crescent of) Ramadan, start fasting, and when you see the (crescent of) Eid al-Fitr, stop fasting. But if the sky is cloudy (i.e., overcast), then estimate for it (i.e., complete thirty days)."
"925","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever does not give up false speech and deceitful actions, Allah has no need for him to leave his food and drink (by merely naming it as fasting)."
"926","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah Almighty says: All the deeds of the son of Adam are for him except fasting; it is for Me and I Myself will reward for it."" And at the end (of the hadith), it was said: ""The fasting person has two joys: one at the time of breaking his fast, he feels happiness, and the other when he meets his Lord, he will be happy upon seeing the reward of his fasting."
"927","Narrated Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he said: ""Whoever among you is able to marry, should marry, for it helps him lower his gaze and guard his private parts (from unlawful acts). And whoever is not able to marry, should fast, for fasting diminishes his sexual power."" (These fasts are not obligatory, but recommended, and the hadith emphasizes this recommendation.)"
"928","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The month is (at least) twenty-nine days, so do not fast until you see the crescent. Then if the sky is overcast (i.e., clouds cover the sky and you cannot see the crescent), complete the count of thirty days."
"929","Narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) that once the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) took an oath of abstention from his pure wives for a month (i.e., swore to abstain from relations). Then, when twenty-nine days had passed, in the morning or after midday, the Prophet (peace be upon him) went to his wives. Someone said to him, ""You had sworn that you would not go to your wives for a month."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) replied, ""A month can also be of twenty-nine days."
"930","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""There are two months that are not reduced; both months are of Eid: (1) Ramadan (2) Dhul-Hijjah."
"931","Narrated by Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""We are an unlettered people; we do not know calculation. (Then, by gesturing with his fingers, he explained that) the month is like this and sometimes like this, meaning sometimes it is twenty-nine days and sometimes thirty days."
"932","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""None of you should fast a day or two before Ramadan. However, if a person has a habit of fasting on a particular day and that day coincides (with these days), then he may fast on that day. (Specifically, it is not permissible to fast with the intention of welcoming Ramadan.)"
"933","Sayyiduna Bara’ (may Allah be pleased with him) says that it was the practice of the companions of Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), that when someone was fasting and, at the time of breaking the fast, slept before having iftar, then for the rest of the night he would not eat anything, nor (the next) day, until (again) evening came. And Sayyiduna Qais bin Sirmah Al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) was fasting, so when the time of iftar came, he went to his wife and asked her if there was anything to eat. She said, “No, but I will go and find something.” And Qais bin Sirmah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to work hard all day, so sleep overcame him (and he fell asleep). When his wife came (with food) and saw him (sleeping), she said, “You have suffered a loss.” The next day, when it was midday, he fainted. This incident was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), so on this occasion this verse was revealed: “It has been made lawful for you to go to your wives during the nights of fasting.” The companions became extremely happy, and then these words of the verse were also revealed: “Eat and drink until the white thread (i.e., the whiteness of dawn) becomes distinct to you from the black thread of the night.”"
"934","Sayyiduna ‘Adi bin Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) says: When the verse: “Until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread...” (Surah Al-Baqarah: 187) was revealed, I took a black rope and a white rope and placed them under my pillow, and during the night I kept looking at them, but I could not discern anything. In the morning, I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and mentioned this to him. He said: “That (black thread) is the darkness of the night, and (white thread) is the whiteness of the morning.”"
"935","Sayyiduna Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We ate suhoor with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he stood up for prayer. (Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) says, I asked him) How much time was there between the adhan and suhoor? He said: As much time as it takes to recite fifty verses."
"936","Narrated Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""(O people!) Eat Suhoor, for there is blessing in Suhoor. (By acting upon the command of the Messenger of Allah, there is practical benefit as well as reward in the Hereafter.)"
"937","Narrated from Sayyiduna Salamah bin Akwa’ (may Allah be pleased with him): (On one occasion) on the day of ‘Ashura, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed a man to announce this: “(Today) whoever has eaten something should not eat any more, and whoever has not eaten should (make the intention of fasting and) keep the fast.”"
"938","It is narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) and Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) that sometimes the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter the morning in a state of janabah after having relations with his wives, then he would perform ghusl and observe the fast."
"939","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) used to kiss (his pure wives) while fasting and used to embrace (i.e., hug), but he (peace be upon him) had more control over his desires than you. (Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) also says that for a fasting person, a woman’s private part is forbidden. Here, “mubasharat” means embracing and body-to-body contact.)"
"940","Narrated by Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""If someone eats or drinks forgetfully, he should complete his fast, for it is Allah who has fed him and given him drink."
"941","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him): (One day) while we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a man came to him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah! I am ruined."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) asked, ""What happened?"" He said, ""I had intercourse with my wife while I was fasting."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Can you free a slave?"" He replied, ""No."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Can you feed sixty poor people?"" He replied, ""No."" (Abu Huraira, may Allah be pleased with him, says) Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) paused. While we were in this state, someone brought a basket made of palm leaves filled with dates to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Where is the questioner?"" He replied, ""Here I am."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Take this basket and give it in charity."" He said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Should I give it to someone poorer than myself? By Allah, there is no household between the two rocky plains of Madinah that is more in need than my household."" Upon hearing this, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) laughed so much that his blessed teeth became visible, then he said, ""Alright! Then feed it to your own family."
"942","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) had cupping done while in the state of Ihram and also had cupping done while fasting."
"943","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We were on a journey with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (when evening came), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: ""Dismount and mix sattu (barley drink) for me."" He said, ""O Messenger of Allah! The sun is still present."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) again said: ""Dismount and mix sattu for me."" He again said, ""O Messenger of Allah! The sun is still present."" So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Dismount and mix sattu for me."" So he dismounted from the camel and mixed sattu for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank it and pointed with his hand towards that direction and said: ""When you see that night has come from this side, then the fasting person should break his fast."
"944","Narrated from Ummul Momineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him), that Hamza bin Amr Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) asked the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""Should I fast while traveling?"" And he used to fast frequently. So the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""If you wish, then fast, and if you wish, then do not fast."
"945","Narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) once set out towards Makkah during Ramadan, and he continuously observed fasting until he reached the place of ""Kudayd."" There, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broke his fast, so the people also broke their fast."
"946","Narrated Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him): We set out on a journey with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during the summer season. The heat was so intense that a person would place his hand on his head due to the severe heat, and none of us was fasting except the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abdullah ibn Rawaha (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"947","Narrated Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey (the expedition of the Conquest), and he saw a crowd and a man over whom shade was being provided. He (the Prophet) said: ""What has happened to him?"" The people replied: ""He is fasting."" So the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""It is not good to fast while traveling (in this condition)."" (Because in this matter, there is an option to fast or not to fast. See the previous hadith in the chapter: Fasting and breaking the fast during travel.)"
"948","Narrated Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): We used to travel with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we observed that the fasting person did not criticize the one who was not fasting, nor did the one who was not fasting criticize the fasting person."
"949","Narrated from Ummul Momineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever dies and has obligatory fasts to make up, his heir should fast on his behalf."
"950","Narrated Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah! My mother has passed away and she had a month's fasts due. Should I fast on her behalf to make up for them?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) replied: ""Yes, fulfilling Allah's debt is most deserving."
"951","The hadith of Sayyiduna Ibn Abi Awfa and the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that ""Dismount and prepare sattu (barley drink) for me"" has already been mentioned (see the previous hadith in the chapter: Fasting and breaking the fast during travel), and in this narration (Sayyiduna Ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him) says that (the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said): ""When you see that the signs of night have appeared from this side, then break your fast."" And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointed with his finger towards the east."
"952","Narrated from Sayyiduna Sahl bin Sa’d (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “The people will remain upon goodness as long as they hasten in breaking the fast.”"
"953","Sayyida Asma bint Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with her) says: Once during the time of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), we broke our fast on a cloudy day (because we could not accurately determine the time), and then the sun appeared afterwards."
"954","Rabi’ bint Mu’awwiz (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a message to the settlements of the Ansar on the morning of ‘Ashura: “Whoever began the morning without fasting should neither eat nor drink for the rest of the day. And whoever began the morning in a state of fasting should complete his fast.” Rabi’ (may Allah be pleased with her) says that after that, we would regularly fast on ‘Ashura and make our children fast as well, and we would make toys out of cotton for them, so that when any of them cried for food, we would give him that toy until the time of iftar came."
"955","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: ""(O people!) Do not observe continuous fasting (without Suhoor and Iftar), and if any of you insists on doing so, then he may do so only until Suhoor."
"956","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade fasting continuously (without breaking the fast at suhoor and iftar). So one of the Muslims said, ""O Messenger of Allah! But you observe continuous fasting (i.e., you keep fasts without breaking them)."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) replied: ""Which one of you is like me? When I sleep at night, my Lord feeds me and gives me drink."" But when they did not refrain, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) observed continuous fasting with them for one day (i.e., ate and drank nothing), then for a second day (i.e., ate and drank nothing), and kept continuous fasts for several days. After that, the (Eid) moon appeared. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), out of displeasure at their refusal, said: ""If the moon had not appeared, I would have made you fast even more."" And in another narration, also from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Take upon yourselves only as much worship as you have the strength for."
"957","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) established brotherhood between Sayyiduna Salman and Sayyiduna Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with them). Because of this, (one day) Salman went to visit Abu Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) and found Umm Darda in a very distressed state. He asked her, ""How are you?"" She replied, ""Your brother Abu Darda has no need for the world anymore."" Meanwhile, Abu Darda also arrived and prepared food for Salman and said to him, ""Eat, I am fasting."" Salman replied, ""I will not eat until you eat as well."" Eventually, Abu Darda broke his fast and ate with Salman. Then, when night came, Abu Darda stood up to pray. Salman said, ""Sleep for now."" So he slept. After a little while, he tried to get up, but Salman said, ""Sleep for now."" Then, when the last part of the night came, Salman said, ""Now get up!"" So both of them prayed. Then Salman said to Abu Darda, ""Indeed, your Lord has a right over you, your soul has a right over you, and your wife has a right over you, so give each one who has a right their due."" Then Abu Darda came to the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) and related the entire incident to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Salman has spoken the truth."" (May Allah be pleased with them all)"
"958","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that when the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) used to observe voluntary fasts, he would fast so much that we would think he would never stop fasting, and when he would stop, he would stop so much that we would think he would never fast again. And I did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) observe a full month of fasting except in Ramadan, and I did not see him fast in any month more than in Sha’ban."
"959","Narrated from Ummul Momineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) did not observe (supererogatory) fasts in any month more than in Sha’ban; rather, it seemed as if he spent almost the whole of Sha’ban fasting. She further says that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say: “O people! Take upon yourselves only as much worship as you can bear, for Allah does not tire (of giving reward) until you tire (of worship), and the best prayer in the sight of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was that which was performed regularly, even if it was little. And whenever the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) performed a prayer, he would make it regular.”"
"960","Sayedna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about the fasting of the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him), so he replied: Whenever I wished to see the Prophet (peace be upon him) fasting in any month, I would see him fasting, and whenever I wished to see him not fasting, I would see him not fasting. And whenever I wished to see him praying at night, I would see him praying, and whenever I wished to see him sleeping (at night), I would see him sleeping. And I have never touched any cloth of fur or silk softer than the blessed hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), nor have I ever found musk or ambergris more fragrant than the scent (of sweat) of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."
"961","The hadith of Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr bin Al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) has already been mentioned earlier (see the Book: Description of the Tahajjud Prayer... Chapter: The person who remained asleep during the last part of the night)."
"962","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates in this narration that when Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) became old, he used to say: ""Alas! If only I had accepted the permission of the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) (to fast three days in every month)."
"963","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr bin al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned the fast of Dawud (peace be upon him) and said: ""He would fast one day and not fast the next day, and when he faced the enemy in the battlefield, he would not flee."" Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) submitted, ""O Messenger of Allah! Is there anyone who can take responsibility for this (i.e., this practice of Dawud, peace be upon him) on my behalf (i.e., so that I may also do this)?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever fasts continuously has not really fasted."" He said this twice. (Ata says that he does not know how the mention of perpetual fasting came here, but he remembered that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this twice.)"
"964","´Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) says that` one day the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) came to Umm Sulaym, so Umm Sulaym placed dates and ghee before him. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Put the ghee back in its container and the dates in their vessel, for I am fasting."" Then the Prophet (peace be upon him) stood in a corner of the house and performed voluntary prayer, and he supplicated for Umm Sulaym and her household. Umm Sulaym said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Have you supplicated only for me?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) replied, ""And for whom else?"" So Umm Sulaym said, ""Supplicate for your servant Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) as well."" Thus, the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not leave out any good of this world and the Hereafter except that he supplicated for me, in this way: ""O Allah! Grant him wealth and children, and bless him."" So see, among the Ansar, I am the wealthiest, and my daughter Amina used to narrate that by the time Hajjaj came to Basra, more than one hundred and twenty of my own children had been buried."
"965","Narrated by Sayyiduna Imran bin Husain (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked a man, ""O father of so-and-so! Did you not fast at the end of this month?"" He replied, ""No, O Messenger of Allah!"" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""When you have finished the (Ramadan) fasts, then make up those fasts."" In another narration, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""Did you not fast two days at the end of Sha'ban?"
"966","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that he was asked whether the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting on Friday. He replied that yes, he forbade it."
"967","Narrated from Umm al-Mu’minin Juwayriya bint al-Harith (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) came to her on a Friday and she was fasting. He (peace be upon him) said: “Did you fast yesterday?” She replied: “No.” Then he (peace be upon him) said: “Do you intend to fast tomorrow?” She again replied: “No.” So he (peace be upon him) said: “(If not) then do not fast today, that is, break your fast.”"
"968","It was asked from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) whether the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to specify certain days for worship. She replied, ""No, the worship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was continuous, and who among you has the strength for what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to have strength for?"
"969","It is narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) and Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that they said: Fasting during the days of Tashreeq was not permitted except for the person who does not have an animal for sacrifice."
"970","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that during the era of ignorance, the Quraysh used to fast on the day of Ashura, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) also used to fast on this day. Then, when the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) arrived in Madinah, he also fasted on this day and ordered the people to do so as well. Then, when the fasting of Ramadan was made obligatory, the fasting of Ashura was abandoned; whoever wished, fasted on Ashura, and whoever wished, did not fast."
"971","Narrated Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, he saw the Jews fasting on the day of Ashura. So he (peace be upon him) asked them: ""What is this (reason that you fast on the day of Ashura)?"" They said: ""This is a great day; it is the day on which Allah saved the Children of Israel from their enemy, so Musa (peace be upon him) used to fast on this day."" So the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""I am more entitled to Musa (peace be upon him) than you."" So he (peace be upon him) fasted on that day and ordered (the Muslims) to fast on it."
